== 1.8 Chapter 1 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [The first pass over an unseen poem is to],
  [look up the poet],
  [count the stanzas],
  [read the questions first],
  [read it aloud slowly]
)

#mcq(2, [The "turn" of a poem is],
  [its title],
  [the point at which it changes direction],
  [the longest line],
  [its final rhyme]
)

#mcq(3, [Which of these is NOT one of the five passes?],
  [Argue],
  [Memorise the poet's dates],
  [Hear],
  [Turn]
)

#mcq(4, [The "voice" pass asks],
  [how many lines the poem has],
  [when the poet was born],
  [who speaks, to whom, and in what tone],
  [what the rhyme scheme is]
)

#mcq(5, [The "image" pass is looking for],
  [the poem's setting],
  [the two or three images that carry the argument],
  [every metaphor in the poem, listed],
  [the poet's other poems]
)

#mcq(6, [The "argue" pass ends with],
  [a list of devices],
  [a paraphrase of every line],
  [a summary of the plot],
  [one sentence stating the claim the whole poem makes]
)

#mcq(7, [The four question shapes are fact, function, voice and tone, and],
  [form and theme],
  [title and date],
  [rhyme and rhythm],
  [biography and history]
)

#mcq(8, [The commonest shape of question on a prescribed poem is],
  [fact],
  [form],
  [biographical],
  [function]
)

#mcq(9, [A function question is answered by naming],
  [how many syllables the line has],
  [what the line does in its stanza],
  [which device the line uses],
  [what the line means]
)

#mcq(10, [The stanza table has four columns: stanza, what happens, what it does, and],
  [the rhyme scheme],
  [a line you can quote],
  [the number of feet],
  [the poet's sources]
)

#mcq(11, [A stanza table for a long narrative poem should have one row per],
  [character],
  [rhyme],
  [scene or episode],
  [line]
)

#mcq(12, [The speaker of a poem is],
  [always a named character],
  [always the poet],
  [a voice constructed by the poet],
  [always the reader]
)

#mcq(13, [The tenor of a metaphor is],
  [the thing being described],
  [the rhythm of the line],
  [the image used to describe something],
  [the poem's theme]
)

#mcq(14, [The vehicle of a metaphor is],
  [the thing being described],
  [the image used to describe it],
  [the stanza form],
  [the tone of voice]
)

#mcq(15, [When a stem quotes half a line, the first move is to],
  [locate the stanza the line comes from],
  [count the syllables],
  [decide the theme],
  [read the four options]
)

#mcq(16, [The best order for answering an unseen poem in the hall is],
  [options first, then poem],
  [questions first, then poem],
  [poem once, then questions, then poem again],
  [poem twice, then questions]
)

#mcq(17, [The last two lines of a lyric usually],
  [introduce a new character],
  [abandon the metre],
  [state or refuse the poem's conclusion],
  [repeat the title]
)

#mcq(18, [A comparison stem is answered by],
  [stating a relation and supporting it from both poems],
  [quoting both titles],
  [choosing the better poem],
  [describing both poems in turn]
)

#mcq(19, [The word that should appear in every comparison answer is],
  [however],
  [whereas],
  [finally],
  [therefore]
)

#mcq(20, ["Both poems are protests" is best answered by],
  [disagreeing outright],
  [agreeing in part and showing where one poem does something else],
  [ignoring the quotation],
  [agreeing and proving it twice]
)

#mcq(21, [An unseen poem of twelve lines can be read twice in],
  [thirty seconds],
  [ten minutes],
  [five seconds],
  [about two minutes]
)

#mcq(22, [The main danger of annotating on the first reading is],
  [missing the poem's movement while hunting details],
  [running out of ink],
  [reading too slowly],
  [spoiling the book]
)

#mcq(23, [Rhyme royal is],
  [four lines rhymed abab],
  [five lines rhymed aabba],
  [unrhymed iambic pentameter],
  [seven lines rhymed ababbcc]
)

#mcq(24, [Free verse is best defined as],
  [poetry without images],
  [poetry without stanzas],
  [poetry without meaning],
  [poetry without a fixed metre or rhyme scheme]
)

#mcq(25, [The tone of a poem is],
  [the poet's biography],
  [its subject],
  [the speaker's attitude as conveyed by the writing],
  [its rhyme scheme]
)

#mcq(26, [A rhetorical question in a poem usually],
  [marks a change of metre],
  [makes a point by not expecting an answer],
  [introduces a character],
  [expects an answer from the reader]
)

#mcq(27, [Anaphora is],
  [a deliberate exaggeration],
  [a comparison using like or as],
  [the repetition of a word or phrase at the start of successive lines],
  [the repetition of a sound at the end of lines]
)

#mcq(28, [Enjambment is],
  [a stanza of four lines],
  [a line that ends with a full stop],
  [a repeated refrain],
  [a sentence running past the end of a line]
)

#mcq(29, [A symbol differs from a metaphor in that a symbol],
  [must rhyme],
  [is explained in the poem],
  [is always a person],
  [stands for something beyond itself and recurs]
)

#mcq(30, [The most economical way to revise five poems in the last week is],
  [re-read all five in full every day],
  [the stanza tables plus the quotation bank plus the traps],
  [learn the poets' biographies],
  [learn the dates of publication]
)

#mcq(31, [A poem's title is best used in an answer as],
  [a substitute for the first line],
  [a date],
  [a claim about the poem's subject that the poem then complicates],
  [decoration]
)

#mcq(32, [Reading a prescribed poem from a summary instead of the text usually fails because],
  [summaries are always wrong],
  [summaries are too short],
  [the paper asks about wording and position, which summaries lose],
  [the text is not examinable]
)

#mcq(33, [In an unseen poem, a change of tense usually signals],
  [a turn, often into memory or consequence],
  [a printing error],
  [the end of the poem],
  [a new speaker]
)

#mcq(34, [The phrase "the poem's persona" refers to],
  [a character in a narrative poem only],
  [the poet's mask or speaking voice],
  [the poem's setting],
  [the rhyme scheme]
)

#mcq(35, [A question that asks "the repetition of 'gently' suggests" is testing],
  [scansion],
  [the poet's dates],
  [the effect of a pattern],
  [fact]
)

#mcq(36, [In a narrative poem, the paper's questions attach mainly to],
  [the rhyme scheme],
  [individual feet],
  [scenes and speeches],
  [the poet's other work]
)

#mcq(37, [A poem's "situation" means],
  [who speaks, where, and to whom],
  [where and when it was published],
  [its theme],
  [its rhyme scheme]
)

#mcq(38, ["Locate before you interpret" means],
  [find the rhyme],
  [find the stanza, then explain the line],
  [find the poet's birthplace],
  [find the theme first]
)

#mcq(39, [The chief use of the answer key in this book is to],
  [explain why the distractors were written as they were],
  [save the reader work],
  [give the right letters],
  [replace the chapter]
)

#mcq(40, [The best way to use the four papers in this volume is],
  [one a week, timed, marked the same day],
  [memorise the answers],
  [all four in one sitting],
  [read the questions only]
)

#shortcut-box("Before you turn to the key")[
  + Mark the whole set in *one pass* — every answer, right or wrong — before you read a single solution.
  + Write the score at the top of the page, because a drill you cannot compare with the last one tells you nothing.
  + Then read *every* solution, including the ones you got right: the second method is in the solution, not in the chapter.
]

